     which means that if a regular expression matches another defined mount point,
     it will override the previously defined threshold.
 
+    A mount can also be configured to alert when its usage drops *below* an
+    expected level, which is usually a sign that the filesystem failed to
+    mount and the mount point is actually just an empty directory on the
+    root filesystem. To configure this, use a mapping with ``minimum``
+    and/or ``maximum`` keys instead of a plain percentage. ``maximum``
+    behaves exactly like the plain percentage form above (alerts when usage
+    is greater than or equal to it), and ``minimum`` alerts when usage drops
+    below it. Both can be set on the same mount, or just one of them.
+
+    .. code-block:: yaml
+
+        beacons:
+          diskusage:
+            - /mnt/nfs:
+                minimum: 10%
+                maximum: 90%
+
     """
     whitelist = []
     config = salt.utils.beacons.remove_hidden_options(config, whitelist)
